<p>If you&rsquo;ve ever been to a Middle Eastern or Mediterranean restaurant, chances are you&rsquo;ve had lavash. Similar to pita, it&rsquo;s the go-to bread for serving with kebabs, dips, and salads. Growing up in Turkey, I learned early on that you simply don&rsquo;t eat kebab without a piece of warm lavash.</p>



<p>Yes, you can find it at the store, but once you taste a freshly cooked, puffed-up lavash straight from the skillet, there&rsquo;s no going back. And the best part? It&rsquo;s easier to make than you think.</p>



<p>In this post, I&rsquo;ll walk you through the step-by-step process of making lavash bread, complete with foolproof tips and troubleshooting advice, so you can confidently make it at home anytime.</p>


<div class="su-note" style="border-color:#d9d8d7;border-radius:1px;-moz-border-radius:1px;-webkit-border-radius:1px;"><div class="su-note-inner su-u-clearfix su-u-trim" style="background-color:#f3f2f1;border-color:#ffffff;color:#000;border-radius:1px;-moz-border-radius:1px;-webkit-border-radius:1px;">



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-what-is-lavash-a-journey-through-this-ancient-flatbread">What is Lavash? A Journey Through This Ancient Flatbread</h2>



<p>Lavash (or <em>lava&#351;</em>, as we call it in Turkey) is a soft, thin flatbread with ancient roots stretching across the Middle East and Central Asia. While the lavash bread recipe may vary slightly from region to region, it is a staple in many countries across the region, including Armenia, Iran, Kazakhstan, Azerbaijan, and Turkey.</p>



<p>At its core, lavash is as simple as bread gets: <strong>just flour, water, salt, sugar, milk (optional), and yeast.</strong> But despite the short ingredient list, the result is anything but basic.&nbsp;</p>



<p>Depending on where it&rsquo;s made, lavash can be thick and chewy or paper-thin and crisp.&nbsp;</p>



<p>My version here sits right in the middle; soft enough to wrap around your favorite kebabs but sturdy enough to use it as a utensil to scoop up dips and salads just like we do in Turkey.</p>


</div></div>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-essential-ingredients-for-lavash-bread">Essential Ingredients for Lavash Bread</h2>



<p>The traditional lavash bread recipes you&rsquo;ll find in cookbooks are usually made with just three ingredients: flour, water, and salt.&nbsp;</p>



<p>While that version is beautiful in its simplicity, it can take a bit more time and technique to get right. In my version, I use a small amount of yeast to help the dough rise more easily, a splash of milk for a softer texture, and a touch of sugar (or, in my case, honey) to feed the yeast and get it bubbling faster.</p>



<p>You&rsquo;ll find the exact measurements of this homemade lavash recipe in the <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/lavash-bread-recipe/#wprm-recipe-container-82304">recipe card below</a>, but here are a few essential notes and tips from my testing to help you get the best results every time.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="800" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-Bread-Ingredient-1200x800.jpg" alt="Ingredients for the recipe are in small bowls from the top view." class="wp-image-82292" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-Bread-Ingredient.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-Bread-Ingredient-350x233.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-Bread-Ingredient-768x512.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-Bread-Ingredient-400x267.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-Bread-Ingredient-150x100.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-Bread-Ingredient.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Water:</strong> We use lukewarm water (around 110 &deg;F &ndash; 43 &deg;C) to activate the yeast. The temperature is important here because if it is colder, the yeast will not activate, and if it is hotter, it will kill it.&nbsp;</p>



<p><strong>Yeast:</strong> I use instant yeast in this recipe, but I still bloom it in warm water with a bit of honey to give it a head start. This step isn&rsquo;t always necessary with instant yeast, but I&rsquo;ve found it helps create a better rise and a more reliable dough.&nbsp;</p>



<p>If you only have active dry yeast, you can use it the same way, just make sure it foams up before moving on.</p>



<p><strong>Sugar:</strong> A small amount of sugar helps feed the yeast and encourages it to bubble up during the resting period. You can use granulated sugar, but I like to use honey to keep this recipe free of refined sugar. Either one will do the job.</p>



<p><strong>Extra Virgin Olive Oil:</strong> I use a little bit of extra virgin olive oil, mostly for flavor. It adds a subtle richness to the dough and helps keep the lavash soft.&nbsp;</p>



<p><strong>Milk:</strong> A small amount of milk makes the dough softer and adds a slightly richer texture. I use whole milk, but any kind you have on hand will work. Just make sure it&rsquo;s at room temperature so it doesn&rsquo;t interfere with the rising time.&nbsp;</p>



<p>If you prefer a dairy-free version, you can use water instead.</p>



<p><strong>All-Purpose Flour vs. Bread Flour:</strong> The recipe I am sharing here is made with all-purpose flour, which yields a soft and pliable dough that is perfect for rolling kebabs. If you prefer a sturdier, chewier texture, you can swap in bread flour instead. It has a higher protein content, which gives the dough more structure.</p>



<p><strong>Kosher salt:</strong> I used Diamond kosher salt, which is less salty than most kosher salts. If you use other brands, such as Morton&rsquo;s, be sure to use half the amount.&nbsp;</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-step-by-step-how-to-make-lavash-bread-at-home">Step-by-Step: How to Make Lavash Bread at Home</h2>



<p>Whenever I make any kind of bread, I always want to know how far in advance to start, especially if I&rsquo;m planning to serve it for dinner. This <strong>easy lavash recipe takes about 1.5 hours from start to finish</strong>, including time to knead, rise, and cook. So if dinner&rsquo;s at 7, starting around 5:30 pm is just right.</p>



<p>This recipe makes 10 lavash, just in case you are planning ahead or cooking for a crowd.</p>



<p>Here&rsquo;s how to make lavash at home, step by step:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Homemade-lavash-recipe.jpg" alt="Yeast is activated and mixed with oil in a bowl." class="wp-image-82293" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Homemade-lavash-recipe.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Homemade-lavash-recipe-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Homemade-lavash-recipe-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Homemade-lavash-recipe-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Homemade-lavash-recipe-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Homemade-lavash-recipe.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 1 &ndash; Activate the Yeast:</strong> Place the lukewarm water (around 110&deg;F / 43&deg;C) and honey (or sugar) in a measuring cup. Stir well to make sure the honey is fully dissolved. Add the yeast and let it sit for 5 minutes, or until you see a grey-ish thick layer of bubbly foam on top. Then stir in the olive oil and milk until fully combined.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/How-to-make-lavash-bread.jpg" alt="Wet ingredients are added into the dry ones and mixed." class="wp-image-82294" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/How-to-make-lavash-bread.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/How-to-make-lavash-bread-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/How-to-make-lavash-bread-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/How-to-make-lavash-bread-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/How-to-make-lavash-bread-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/How-to-make-lavash-bread.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 2 &ndash; Combine Wet &amp; Dry Ingredients:</strong> In a large mixing bowl, combine the flour and salt. Pour in the yeast mixture and knead the dough with clean hands for about 5 minutes, or until no dry flour remains.&nbsp;</p>



<p>At this point, the dough (and your hands) will be sticky, and that is totally normal. As you knead, turn the dough frequently to ensure everything comes together evenly.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Iranian-lavash-bread.jpg" alt="The bread dough risen and then kneaded." class="wp-image-82295" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Iranian-lavash-bread.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Iranian-lavash-bread-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Iranian-lavash-bread-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Iranian-lavash-bread-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Iranian-lavash-bread-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Iranian-lavash-bread.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 3 &ndash; First Rise:</strong> Cover the bowl with a clean kitchen towel (or loosely with plastic wrap) and let it rise in a warm part of your kitchen for 20 minutes. By the end of that time, it should look a little smoother and slightly puffed in volume.</p>



<p><strong>Step 4 &ndash; Knead the Dough:</strong> Lightly flour your kitchen counter and transfer the dough over. Knead it for about 5 minutes.</p>



<p>You don&rsquo;t really need much of a technique here, but when I&rsquo;m kneading it, I usually press the whole dough with the heel of my hand, push it away, fold it back over, and give it a turn. Then I just keep repeating that motion until the dough feels smooth and stretchy.</p>



<p>It shouldn&rsquo;t be too sticky, but if it is, go ahead and sprinkle in a bit more flour, just a little at a time (no more than a tablespoon). You don&rsquo;t want to use too much flour, as this can make your lavash too dry or stiff.</p>



<p>Alternatively, you can knead the dough in a stand mixer using the dough hook. However, keep in mind that you might not need to knead it as long. The visual cue to pay attention to is for the dough to be smooth and no longer sticking to the sides.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-style-flatbread.jpg" alt="The dough rolled and cut into the balls." class="wp-image-82296" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-style-flatbread.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-style-flatbread-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-style-flatbread-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-style-flatbread-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-style-flatbread-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Lavash-style-flatbread.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 5 &ndash; Divide the Dough:</strong> Shape the dough into a log approximately 12 inches long and divide it into 10 equal pieces. When you go with 10, each lavash ends up being about 9 to 10 inches in diameter once it is rolled out and cooked. If your skillet is smaller, you can divide the dough into 12 and make slightly smaller ones.</p>



<p>You can totally eyeball it, but if you want all your lavash to be about the same size, a kitchen scale helps.</p>



<p>Grab a dinner plate and sprinkle it with a little flour. Roll each piece into a ball and place them on the plate. Then cover them loosely with a kitchen towel and let them rest for 20 minutes.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Easy-lavash-recipe.jpg" alt="The lavash dough is rolled out." class="wp-image-82297" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Easy-lavash-recipe.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Easy-lavash-recipe-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Easy-lavash-recipe-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Easy-lavash-recipe-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Easy-lavash-recipe-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Easy-lavash-recipe.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 6 &ndash; Heat the Skillet &amp; Roll Out the Dough:</strong> When you are ready to cook, heat a large (10- or 12-inch) nonstick or cast-iron skillet over high heat until it is really hot. This part is essential because if the pan isn&rsquo;t hot enough, the dough will stick. You&rsquo;ll know it is ready when a few drops of water sizzle and disappear almost instantly.</p>



<p>While the pan heats up, start rolling out the dough. Take one ball at a time and roll it into a 9- to 10-inch circle using a rolling pin. Do your best to keep it round, but I&rsquo;ll be honest; it is a little tricky at first.&nbsp;</p>



<p>The good news? It gets easier the more you do it, and honestly, a slightly wonky lavash still tastes just as good.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lavash-bread.jpg" alt="The lavash cooked in a skillet from the top view." class="wp-image-82298" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lavash-bread.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lavash-bread-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lavash-bread-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lavash-bread-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lavash-bread-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lavash-bread.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 7 &ndash; Cook the Lavash:&nbsp;</strong>Before you start rolling, grab a basket (a tortilla warmer works beautifully if you have one) or a bowl and line it with a kitchen towel. We need a place to store the freshly cooked lavash and keep it soft and warm. Set that aside.</p>



<p>Carefully transfer the rolled-out dough to the hot skillet. Within 10 to 15 seconds, you should see little bubbles start to form on the surface. Give it a flip, cook for another 10 seconds, then flip again. As it cooks and puffs, you&rsquo;ll see golden brown spots start to appear, which is exactly what we want.</p>



<p>Keep flipping every 10-15 seconds, and cook for about 40-50 seconds total, or until both sides are cooked and it has some nice color.</p>



<p>Transfer the freshly cooked lavash to your prepared basket and cover it with the towel to keep it warm. Then just repeat the process (roll, cook, and store) for the remaining dough balls.</p>


<div class="su-note" style="border-color:#d9d8d7;border-radius:1px;-moz-border-radius:1px;-webkit-border-radius:1px;"><div class="su-note-inner su-u-clearfix su-u-trim" style="background-color:#f3f2f1;border-color:#ffffff;color:#000;border-radius:1px;-moz-border-radius:1px;-webkit-border-radius:1px;">



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-expert-tips-nbsp">Expert Tips&nbsp;</h2>



<p><strong>Roll it out thin:</strong> The key to getting soft, pliable lavash is rolling the dough really thin. I know it is tempting to focus on making a perfect circle, but honestly, thinness matters more than shape. Even if your lavash isn&rsquo;t perfectly round, it will still taste great.</p>



<p>And if you have a pasta machine, that is a great shortcut to get an even, thin sheet of dough without too much effort.</p>



<p><strong>Go easy on the extra flour:</strong> It is tempting to keep adding flour if the dough feels sticky, but try to trust the process and the visual cues I mentioned earlier. Only add more flour when the dough really starts to stick (especially while rolling), and try to work quickly. Too much flour can lead to stiff, dry lavash, and we definitely don&rsquo;t want that.</p>



<p><strong>Keep it warm:</strong> Don&rsquo;t skip setting up a spot to keep the cooked lavash warm. Otherwise, while you are busy cooking the rest, the earlier ones will cool down and lose their soft, pliable texture. And you don&rsquo;t need anything fancy; a basket or a bowl lined with a clean kitchen towel works perfectly.</p>



<p><strong>Work faster with a buddy: </strong>The most time-consuming part of this recipe is rolling and cooking the lavash. But it goes a lot quicker (and is honestly more fun) if you have someone helping out. I usually rope my husband in. He is in charge of the skillets and cooks two at a time while I roll out the dough. Teamwork makes it so much easier.</p>



<p><strong>Stay close while it cooks: </strong>These flatbreads cook fast, so it is important to keep a close eye on them, even while you are multitasking. I know you&rsquo;ll likely be rolling out the next one while one is cooking (I do the same), but try not to turn your back on the skillet. It gets very hot, and things move quickly, so just stay aware, especially if you have young kids around.</p>


</div></div>
</div>



