<div id="recipe-82304-notes" class="wprm-recipe-notes-container wprm-block-text-normal"><h3 class="wprm-recipe-header wprm-recipe-notes-header wprm-block-text-bold wprm-align-left wprm-header-decoration-none" style="">Notes</h3><div class="wprm-recipe-notes"><ul>
<li><strong>Yields:</strong> This recipe yields 10 lavash, each approximately 9 to 10 inches in diameter. The nutritional values below are per piece.</li>
<li><strong>Storage:&nbsp;</strong>Bring the leftover bread to room temperature and store in an airtight container (or a Ziploc bag) for up to 4 days.</li>
<li><strong>Reheating:&nbsp;</strong>You can reheat in a low-heat oven (325&deg;F) for 8-10 minutes, or until thoroughly warmed through. Or you can wrap it in a lightly wetted paper towel and microwave for 30 seconds.&nbsp;</li>
<li><strong>A word on the yeast:</strong> I used instant rapid yeast as I find that it blooms faster but active dry yeast can also be used.</li>
<li>This recipe was adapted from Betul Tunc&rsquo;s cookbook Turkuaz Kitchen&rsquo;s lavash recipe with minor changes.</li>
